<section class="page-hero">
  <div id="ambient-canvas" class="ambient-canvas"></div>
  <div class="container page-hero-inner">
    <span class="eyebrow">Free Tool</span>
    <h1>Free SERP Snippet Preview Tool</h1>
    <p>This <strong>free SERP snippet preview tool</strong> shows exactly how your title and meta description will look in Google search results, with live character-count warnings, before you publish. No signup, nothing leaves your browser.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="container contact-grid llms-tool-grid">
    <div>
      <form class="contact-form llms-form" id="serp-form" onsubmit="return false;" aria-label="SERP snippet preview form">
        <div>
          <label for="sp-url">Page URL</label>
          <input type="text" id="sp-url" placeholder="https://example.com/blog/roof-repair-cost-guide" value="https://example.com/blog/roof-repair-cost-guide">
        </div>
        <div>
          <label for="sp-title">Page title <span id="sp-title-count" class="llms-status"></span></label>
          <input type="text" id="sp-title" placeholder="Roof Repair Cost Guide: 2026 Pricing by Damage Type" value="Roof Repair Cost Guide: 2026 Pricing by Damage Type">
        </div>
        <div>
          <label for="sp-desc">Meta description <span id="sp-desc-count" class="llms-status"></span></label>
          <textarea id="sp-desc" placeholder="See real 2026 roof repair costs broken down by damage type, roof material, and region, plus what actually drives the price up or down.">See real 2026 roof repair costs broken down by damage type, roof material, and region, plus what actually drives the price up or down.</textarea>
        </div>
        <div>
          <label>Preview mode</label>
          <div style="display:flex; gap:10px;">
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-outline btn-sm" id="sp-desktop-btn" data-mode="desktop">Desktop</button>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-outline btn-sm" id="sp-mobile-btn" data-mode="mobile">Mobile</button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </form>
    </div>

    <div class="contact-info-card llms-preview-card">
      <div class="llms-preview-head">
        <strong>Google preview</strong>
        <span id="sp-status" class="llms-status">Live</span>
      </div>
      <div id="sp-preview" class="serp-preview">
        <div class="serp-site-row">
          <img id="sp-favicon" class="serp-favicon" alt="" width="20" height="20">
          <span id="sp-preview-sitename" class="serp-sitename"></span>
        </div>
        <div id="sp-preview-url" class="serp-url"></div>
        <div id="sp-preview-title" class="serp-title"></div>
        <div id="sp-preview-desc" class="serp-desc"></div>
      </div>
      <p class="llms-hint">This is an approximation — Google truncates by pixel width, not character count, and can rewrite your title or description entirely.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="container llms-explainer">

    <h2>Why a SERP Snippet Preview Matters</h2>
    <p>Your title tag and meta description are the ad copy for your page — Google decides whether to rank you, but real people decide whether to click based on what they see in that snippet. A title that's a few characters too long gets cut off mid-word with an ellipsis, which looks unfinished and hurts click-through rate even on a page that ranks well.</p>

    <h2>Recommended Length Guidelines</h2>
    <div style="overflow-x: auto;">
      <table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:16px 0; font-size:0.95rem;">
        <thead>
          <tr style="border-bottom:2px solid currentColor;">
            <th style="text-align:left; padding:10px 8px;">Element</th>
            <th style="text-align:left; padding:10px 8px;">Recommended length</th>
            <th style="text-align:left; padding:10px 8px;">What happens if you go over</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(128,128,128,0.3);"><td style="padding:10px 8px;">Title tag</td><td style="padding:10px 8px;">50&#8211;60 characters</td><td style="padding:10px 8px;">Truncated with an ellipsis, sometimes mid-word</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="padding:10px 8px;">Meta description</td><td style="padding:10px 8px;">150&#8211;160 characters</td><td style="padding:10px 8px;">Cut off, or Google may replace it with page text entirely</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>These are guidelines, not hard rules — Google measures by pixel width, so a title full of narrow letters (like "i" and "l") can run longer than one full of wide letters (like "W" and "M") before it truncates.</p>

    <h2>What Google Might Rewrite Anyway</h2>
    <ul class="service-points">
      <li><strong>Titles that don't match the page's actual content</strong> — Google increasingly rewrites titles using on-page H1s or content when it decides the tag is misleading or vague</li>
      <li><strong>Duplicate or templated titles</strong> across many pages on the same site</li>
      <li><strong>Meta descriptions that don't answer the query</strong> — Google often pulls a different snippet from the page body when this happens</li>
      <li><strong>Keyword-stuffed titles</strong> that read as unnatural to Google's quality systems</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to Write a Title That Won't Get Cut Off</h2>
    <ul class="service-points">
      <li>Put your primary keyword and most important word near the start, not buried at the end</li>
      <li>Keep it under 60 characters when possible, and always check the live preview before publishing</li>
      <li>Avoid stacking multiple keywords separated by pipes just to fit more terms in</li>
      <li>Write it for a human first — a title that reads naturally survives Google's rewrite systems better than one that reads like a keyword list</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Free SERP Snippet Preview FAQs</h2>
    <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top: 22px;">
      <details>
        <summary>Is this exactly how my page will look in Google?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>It's a close approximation, not a guarantee. Google truncates by actual pixel width rather than character count, and can rewrite your title or description at will if it decides something else better matches the search query.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Why does Google sometimes show a different description than what I wrote?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Google dynamically generates the snippet it thinks best answers a specific search query, which can mean pulling a different sentence from your page body instead of your written meta description — especially for long-tail or highly specific queries.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does the meta description affect my search ranking?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Not directly as a ranking factor, but it strongly affects click-through rate, and click-through rate is one of the signals Google's systems can pick up on over time.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Should mobile and desktop titles be different?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>You only write one title tag — Google renders the same one on both, but mobile results generally show slightly less horizontal space, so a title optimized for desktop can occasionally truncate a bit earlier on mobile.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does this tool store or send my content anywhere?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. Everything runs in your browser with JavaScript — nothing is uploaded, logged, or stored on any server, including ours.</p>
      </details>
    </div>

    <div class="cta-band" style="margin-top: 44px;">
      <span class="cta-orb cta-orb-1"></span>
      <span class="cta-orb cta-orb-2"></span>
      <h2>Want every page's on-page SEO handled?</h2>
      <p>Contomatix audits and fixes titles, metas, and structure across your whole site.</p>
      <div class="cta-actions">
        <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-primary">Get in touch</a>
        <a href="https://wa.me/<%= site.whatsappNumber %>" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-glass">Chat on WhatsApp</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

